.TH "digitalEvents" 3tcl 0\&.5 "ngspicetclbridge" "Tcl NgspiceTclBridge package"
.SH NAME
digitalEvents - Commands in namespace digitalEvents
.SH "DIGITAL EVENTS DATA"
.SH "DIGITAL EVENTS DATA"
.PP
Digital XSPICE nodes produce string values rather than numeric vectors\&. Their data is collected separately
from analog voltages/currents, regardless of the handle's -output setting\&. The raw event API must be present
in the loaded ngspice library, and the circuit's XSPICE code models must be loaded\&.
.PP
Load the circuit, select the nodes and publication mode, then run the simulation:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set sim [::ngspicetclbridge::new $ngspiceLibrary]
$sim circuit -string $netlist
$sim eventnodes -all -mode changes
::ngspicetclbridge::run $sim
set data [$sim eventdata]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use -nodes {clk out} instead of -all to record particular nodes\&. Calling eventnodes without options
returns supported, available, selected and mode\&. Discovery and configuration require an idle simulator\&. An
empty -nodes {} selection disables recording\&. Configuration changes clear the stored history and known states\&.
.PP
eventdata returns the currently retained history, not just records added since the previous read\&. Every
result has four top-level keys: mode, analysis, nodes and batches\&. It is an independent Tcl value; subsequent runs,
callbacks and clears do not change a result already returned to your script\&. No Tcl event-loop turn is
required to read the published data\&. Event output does not use RBC vectors or vector naming options\&.
.PP
analysis is none before a plot starts, then tran, op or dc\&. Starting another analysis replaces the current
event result\&. AC/noise and other unsupported plots cause eventdata to report an error; their operating-point
preparation is not presented as a frequency-dependent digital response\&.
.SS "CHOOSING A TRANSIENT MODE"
.TP
\(bu
changes: record native event reports grouped into complete sampling batches\&. Use it for transition logs\&.
.TP
\(bu
snapshot: record all selected node states at each accepted transient output point\&. Use it for aligned state tables; unchanged values are carried forward\&.
.TP
\(bu
pernode: record each node's native event reports as soon as they arrive\&. Use it for individual waveforms or monitoring that must not wait for a complete batch across nodes\&.
.PP
The following outputs are illustrative, formatted across lines for readability\&. Times are in seconds for
transient analysis\&. Value strings are supplied by ngspice's event-type decoder: for example, digital values can
contain logic state and drive strength, such as 0s, 1s or Uz\&. Preserve the complete string; other event
types may use different representations\&. The type field names the native event type, such as digital\&.
.SS "CHANGES: COMPLETE BATCHES OF EVENT REPORTS"
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
mode changes
analysis tran
nodes {clk {type digital} out {type digital}}
batches {
    {time 1e-9 events {{clk 1e-9 1s 1} {out 1e-9 0s 1}}}
    {time 2e-9 events {{out 1\&.5e-9 1s 1}}}
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
nodes maps each selected node name to its metadata dictionary\&. batches is an ordered list of dictionaries:
.TP
\(bu
time: the transient output point associated with this batch\&.
.TP
\(bu
events: records in the form {node eventTime value last}\&. node is the original ngspice node name; eventTime is the time of that native event, which may differ from the batch time; value is its string\&.
.TP
\(bu
last: 1 marks the last report in that particular node's native callback group; 0 means more reports for that node follow\&. It does not indicate that all nodes have been delivered, or that simulation has ended\&.
.PP
Empty batches are omitted\&. Native predecessor records, repeated timestamps and repeated values are
preserved, so changes does not mean every row has a value different from the previous row\&. Records retain
callback order; events from different nodes are not necessarily globally sorted by eventTime\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
foreach batch [dict get $data batches] {
    foreach event [dict get $batch events] {
        lassign $event node eventTime value last
        puts "$node at $eventTime: $value"
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SS "SNAPSHOT: ALL SELECTED STATES AT EACH OUTPUT POINT"
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
mode snapshot
analysis tran
nodes {clk {type digital} out {type digital}}
batches {
    {time 1e-9 values {clk 1s out {}} known {clk}}
    {time 2e-9 values {clk 1s out 0s} known {clk out}}
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each batch contains time, values and known:
.TP
\(bu
time: the accepted transient output time for this snapshot\&.
.TP
\(bu
values: a dictionary containing every selected node and its latest received value\&. Nodes without a new report retain their previous value; a node with no report yet has an empty string\&.
.TP
\(bu
known: the names of nodes whose values have actually been received\&. Consult this list to distinguish a missing initial value from an explicit unknown/high-impedance state reported by ngspice\&.
.PP
Registering a node does not force ngspice to report an initial value\&. In the first example snapshot, out has
not reported yet; in the second it has reported 0s, while clk is carried forward as 1s\&. Snapshots record
one batch per accepted output point, even if nothing changes\&. This can require much more memory than changes or
pernode\&. The snapshot is a carried-forward state, not an interpolation of the digital history\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
foreach batch [dict get $data batches] {
    set time [dict get $batch time]
    if {{out} in [dict get $batch known]} {
        puts "$time: [dict get $batch values out]"
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SS "PERNODE: IMMEDIATELY AVAILABLE NODE HISTORIES"
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
mode pernode
analysis tran
nodes {
    clk {type digital events {{0\&.0 0s 0} {1e-9 1s 1}}}
    out {type digital events {{1\&.5e-9 0s 1}}}
}
batches {}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each node dictionary adds events, a list of {eventTime value last} records\&. The node name is already the
dictionary key, so it is omitted from each record\&. These fields have the same meanings as in changes\&.
batches is always empty\&. A selected node with no reports has events {}\&. Histories for different nodes
can be at different stages of delivery during a run; this mode provides no all-nodes completion boundary\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
foreach event [dict get $data nodes clk events] {
    lassign $event eventTime value last
    puts "$eventTime: $value"
}
.fi
.RE
.SS "OPERATING POINT AND DC SWEEP"
.PP
The same eventnodes and eventdata commands support OP and DC\&. Selection, clear, ownership and circuit
replacement rules are unchanged\&. The bridge samples settled node histories at each numeric output point,
including internal subcircuit nodes\&. OP/DC points are available immediately after that callback, without
the one-output-point publication delay used for transient batches\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
$sim eventnodes -all -mode snapshot
$sim command op
set operatingPoint [$sim eventdata]
$sim command {dc vin 0 2 1}
set sweep [$sim eventdata]
.fi
.RE
.PP
For an ADC bridge with thresholds 0\&.5 V and 1\&.5 V, a DC snapshot result can look like this:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
mode snapshot
analysis dc
nodes {xlogic\&.d {type d}}
batches {
    {point 0 coordinates {vin 0\&.0} values {xlogic\&.d 0s} known {xlogic\&.d}}
    {point 1 coordinates {vin 1\&.0} values {xlogic\&.d Us} known {xlogic\&.d}}
    {point 2 coordinates {vin 2\&.0} values {xlogic\&.d 1s} known {xlogic\&.d}}
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
\(bu
point is the zero-based numeric output index in this plot\&. It continues across nested sweep branches and is not reset by eventdata -clear\&. It is not a simulation timestamp\&.
.TP
\(bu
coordinates maps sweep names to values in native units: volts, amperes, ohms or degrees Celsius\&. An OP result has analysis op and one point with coordinates {}\&. It has no time coordinate\&.
.TP
\(bu
values maps every selected node to its settled string state\&. known lists the nodes successfully read; a native U state is a known reported value, not missing data\&.
.PP
For a nested sweep such as dc vin 0 0\&.4 0\&.2 vbias 0 0\&.2 0\&.2, the last snapshot has point 5 and coordinates
{vin 0\&.4 vbias 0\&.2}\&. The primary coordinate comes from ngspice's output scale\&. The outer coordinate starts at
the declared outer limit and advances when the primary scale wraps to its next branch\&. Repeated primary values
therefore remain distinguishable\&. Nested sweep limits must be literal numbers, optionally with SPICE suffixes;
parameter expressions, altered sweep definitions and compressed/filtered output are not supported\&. Use one
\&.dc directive in circuit input, or a direct dc command; includes, aliases and multiple \&.dc directives
cannot reliably provide coordinate metadata and are outside this interface\&. A retrieval failure is reported by
eventdata rather than returning a partly filled point\&.
.PP
OP/DC changes mode records the first known state and subsequent value changes\&. Empty points are omitted:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
mode changes
analysis dc
nodes {xlogic\&.d {type d}}
batches {
    {point 0 coordinates {vin 0\&.0} events {{xlogic\&.d 0s}}}
    {point 1 coordinates {vin 1\&.0} events {{xlogic\&.d Us}}}
    {point 2 coordinates {vin 2\&.0} events {{xlogic\&.d 1s}}}
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
OP/DC pernode mode stores those changes under each node\&. Each record is a dictionary:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
mode pernode
analysis dc
nodes {xlogic\&.d {type d events {
    {point 0 coordinates {vin 0\&.0} value 0s}
    {point 1 coordinates {vin 1\&.0} value Us}
    {point 2 coordinates {vin 2\&.0} value 1s}
}}}
batches {}
.fi
.RE
.PP
OP uses the same mode-dependent layouts, with only the initial state and an empty coordinates dictionary\&.
There is no last flag in OP/DC records: these are settled states, not native transient callback groups\&.
No transient delay, pulse width or glitch history can be inferred from a DC sweep\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
foreach batch [dict get $sweep batches] {
    puts "[dict get $batch coordinates vin] V: [dict get $batch values xlogic\&.d]"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
OP/DC collection uses ngGet_Evt_NodeInfo\&. Queries copy native event history, so their cost
grows with stored history; this initial implementation favors correctness over large-sweep performance\&.
.SS "PUBLICATION, CLEARING AND REPEATED RUNS"
.PP
All transient modes receive data through the same raw callback\&. In changes and snapshot, the next analog sample
callback publishes the preceding batch; completion or halt publishes the final batch\&. The raw callback's
last flag alone cannot complete a batch across all nodes\&. Reading eventdata or calling flush does not
publish a partial batch\&. Purely digital transient circuits use ngspice's time-only output for this boundary\&.
Transient collection requires normal output from time zero; there is no wall-clock sampling interval\&.
.PP
eventdata -clear discards committed and pending history, retaining node selection and latest known states\&.
It returns the cleared result, not the discarded records\&. Later callbacks continue recording; snapshot can
carry forward retained states\&. To consume history, read it before clearing it\&. Reads and clears are separate
calls: pause the simulation first if records arriving between those calls must not be discarded\&.
.PP
A new plot clears history and known states\&. Halt/resume of the same plot preserves them\&. Numeric vectors
-clear and command -clear do not clear digital history\&. Replacing a circuit through the circuit subcommand
while idle clears history and explicit -nodes selection, retains the mode, and re-registers -all against the
new nodes\&. Direct circuit-changing commands and scripts are unsupported once event collection is configured\&.
.PP
eventcounts exposes send_raw_evt (native callback invocations), event_init (bridge initialization) and
event_batch (complete transient batch boundaries or accepted OP/DC output points)\&. All support waitevent
-since\&. event_batch includes boundaries with no changes, so it need not equal the number of stored changes
batches\&. Disabled node registrations remain until circuit destruction and can still contribute to
send_raw_evt\&. Clearing history does not reset these counters\&. Decoder errors are reported by eventdata
until configuration or a new plot resets them\&.


